WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.787
Welcome back to Building
the Daily Checks Workbook.

2
00:00:02.787 --> 00:00:05.750
This is Chapter 3: what
each tab would show.

3
00:00:05.750 --> 00:00:10.502
An empty queue over a source that stopped
arriving looks exactly like a quiet night,

4
00:00:10.502 --> 00:00:13.463
and the renderer in this
chapter says which one it is.

5
00:00:13.463 --> 00:00:17.163
We'll see what Microsoft Learn
and the operator runbook give you,

6
00:00:17.163 --> 00:00:21.839
the renderer's rules, the read-only
reachability check and the replication probe,

7
00:00:21.839 --> 00:00:24.771
then walk two mornings in
the fictional workspace,

8
00:00:24.771 --> 00:00:27.563
one of them with a domain
controller gone silent.

9
00:00:27.563 --> 00:00:30.334
The chapter has been
reviewed and approved,

10
00:00:30.334 --> 00:00:34.160
and the code is illustrative,
tested against fixtures.

11
00:00:37.910 --> 00:00:39.023
Why this chapter?

12
00:00:39.023 --> 00:00:42.023
A dashboard's most
dangerous state isn't red.

13
00:00:42.023 --> 00:00:44.547
It's green over a source
that stopped arriving.

14
00:00:44.547 --> 00:00:47.629
A risk queue with no rows
can mean nobody is at risk,

15
00:00:47.629 --> 00:00:50.860
or that the sign-in logs
stopped coming in last night.

16
00:00:50.860 --> 00:00:53.997
By the end of the chapter you
can show, without a tenant,

17
00:00:53.997 --> 00:00:56.706
what each tab would
display on a given morning;

18
00:00:56.706 --> 00:01:01.198
tell an operator which tabs can't be
trusted because their source went quiet;

19
00:01:01.198 --> 00:01:04.335
and prove each tab's tables
answer in a real workspace.

20
00:01:04.335 --> 00:01:06.803
It's for developers, security operations,

21
00:01:06.803 --> 00:01:10.370
and the operators who read
the workbook each morning.

22
00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:15.395
Here's what you start with.

23
00:01:15.395 --> 00:01:20.125
The operator runbook's completeness
check lists each expected table past its

24
00:01:20.125 --> 00:01:22.600
freshness limit, and
it uses a fuzzy union,

25
00:01:22.600 --> 00:01:26.457
so a missing table shows up as a
null instead of failing the query.

26
00:01:26.457 --> 00:01:30.781
Sentinel's health page gives the query
for each connector's latest failure,

27
00:01:30.781 --> 00:01:34.269
and recommends Azure Monitor
alert rules for health drifts.

28
00:01:34.269 --> 00:01:38.652
Microsoft's Intune page says compliance
is exported once a day and can

29
00:01:38.652 --> 00:01:42.958
take up to forty-eight hours, and not
to rely on a fixed completion time.

30
00:01:42.958 --> 00:01:46.557
The Azure command line can run
a query against a workspace,

31
00:01:46.557 --> 00:01:48.657
from its log analytics extension.

32
00:01:48.657 --> 00:01:53.245
Learn's unavailable-computers query
takes each computer's latest heartbeat.

33
00:01:53.245 --> 00:01:58.337
The replication report gives each link's
last success, last failure and status,

34
00:01:58.337 --> 00:02:02.645
and reading it forest-wide needs
Enterprise Admins or equivalent.

35
00:02:02.645 --> 00:02:05.285
And the Logs Ingestion API takes a list of

36
00:02:05.285 --> 00:02:08.510
records posted to a
collection rule's stream.

37
00:02:12.260 --> 00:02:14.747
Here are the renderer's rules, in order.

38
00:02:14.747 --> 00:02:16.761
A link-out tab shows LINK-OUT.

39
00:02:16.761 --> 00:02:19.560
No query result at all shows NO DATA.

40
00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:23.400
If any source the tab depends
on is past its freshness limit,

41
00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:27.710
the tab shows STALE, and says the
rows it does show may be incomplete.

42
00:02:27.710 --> 00:02:31.385
Otherwise, any row the tab's
rule flags shows ATTENTION.

43
00:02:31.385 --> 00:02:36.260
No rows where a row is expected, like
the morning probe, shows NO DATA.

44
00:02:36.260 --> 00:02:38.147
And only then, CLEAR.

45
00:02:38.147 --> 00:02:40.623
So a stale source never renders clear.

46
00:02:40.623 --> 00:02:45.850
The render carries the banner, and the
kit refuses to record it as evidence.

47
00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:50.825
What does it leave to you?

48
00:02:50.825 --> 00:02:53.564
The other tabs don't know
their table is dark,

49
00:02:53.564 --> 00:02:57.900
so each tab names its own sources and
limits, and says what empty means.

50
00:02:57.900 --> 00:03:02.962
Intune's timing is variable, so the
devices tab allows forty-eight hours.

51
00:03:02.962 --> 00:03:05.596
A screenshot is easy to file as evidence,

52
00:03:05.596 --> 00:03:09.187
so the render carries the
banner and the kit refuses it.

53
00:03:09.187 --> 00:03:13.954
Nothing proves each tab's tables
answer, so the kit asks the workspace

54
00:03:13.954 --> 00:03:18.637
for each table's newest record and
count: delivering, late or missing.

55
00:03:18.637 --> 00:03:23.630
Learn's unavailable-computers query lists
only servers that once reported, so the

56
00:03:23.630 --> 00:03:28.549
sync and Active Directory tabs start from
the settings' server lists, one row each.

57
00:03:28.549 --> 00:03:32.648
And the replication report is a
file in local time on one host,

58
00:03:32.648 --> 00:03:36.009
so the kit's probe turns it
into one record per link,

59
00:03:36.009 --> 00:03:40.390
with a verdict and times in U T
C, and sends it to the workspace.

60
00:03:44.140 --> 00:03:45.065
Who does what?

61
00:03:45.065 --> 00:03:47.731
Operators read the telemetry tab first,

62
00:03:47.731 --> 00:03:51.690
then their own family's tabs,
and each finding becomes a task.

63
00:03:51.690 --> 00:03:56.390
Security operations treats each dark
source as a high-severity task.

64
00:03:56.390 --> 00:04:01.552
And the security officer keeps the reachability
and probe records, never the render.

65
00:04:01.552 --> 00:04:04.102
Lab 1 needs only saved results.

66
00:04:04.102 --> 00:04:07.382
Lab 2 needs Log Analytics
Reader on a test workspace,

67
00:04:07.382 --> 00:04:12.340
which can view and search the monitoring
data, and the check sends only queries.

68
00:04:12.340 --> 00:04:18.005
The live replication probe is different:
reading replication needs Enterprise

69
00:04:18.005 --> 00:04:23.440
Admins or equivalent, so Chapter 4 runs
it only on a Tier 0 host, or not at all.

70
00:04:27.190 --> 00:04:28.002
Lab 1.

71
00:04:28.002 --> 00:04:33.016
Step 1 renders the morning of November
sixteenth in the fictional workspace.

72
00:04:33.016 --> 00:04:36.082
The Entra diagnostic setting
stopped at ten past five

73
00:04:36.082 --> 00:04:38.740
the evening before, and
the sync probe didn't run.

74
00:04:38.740 --> 00:04:44.084
Telemetry flags four rows: sign-in and
audit logs are dark, the probe's table

75
00:04:44.084 --> 00:04:49.165
is dark, and the Office 365 connector's
latest health event is a failure.

76
00:04:49.165 --> 00:04:54.724
The sync, risk, emergency access, grants
and admin changes tabs are all STALE,

77
00:04:54.724 --> 00:04:58.490
even the sync server's two
overnight export errors.

78
00:04:58.490 --> 00:05:01.981
The Active Directory tab's sources
are fresh, so it can be read:

79
00:05:01.981 --> 00:05:05.960
the third domain controller went
silent at seven the evening before,

80
00:05:05.960 --> 00:05:07.915
and both links from it are failing.

81
00:05:07.915 --> 00:05:10.770
Step 2 shows why the risk tab isn't clear:

82
00:05:10.770 --> 00:05:14.790
no rows, but its source
stopped fourteen hours ago.

83
00:05:18.540 --> 00:05:20.728
Step 3 renders the next morning.

84
00:05:20.728 --> 00:05:23.577
The setting is back,
and telemetry is clear.

85
00:05:23.577 --> 00:05:27.270
Now the queues show what they were
hiding: one user at high risk,

86
00:05:27.270 --> 00:05:32.192
one emergency access sign-in, which turns
out to be the quarterly validation drill,

87
00:05:32.192 --> 00:05:36.390
and the review records it as a drill,
and one synchronization conflict.

88
00:05:36.390 --> 00:05:41.190
The staging synchronization server wrote
no backup event, so it's flagged too.

89
00:05:41.190 --> 00:05:45.491
The admin changes tab lists yesterday's
five request-driven changes,

90
00:05:45.491 --> 00:05:47.453
each to be matched to its ticket.

91
00:05:47.453 --> 00:05:50.595
Grants, D L P alerts and
Active Directory are clear,

92
00:05:50.595 --> 00:05:55.320
and this time clear means something,
because their sources are fresh.

93
00:05:59.070 --> 00:06:04.307
Step 4 runs the replication probe on the
first morning's saved replication report.

94
00:06:04.307 --> 00:06:07.945
Without the send switch, it writes
its file and sends nothing.

95
00:06:07.945 --> 00:06:10.983
Four links between the
fictional domain controllers.

96
00:06:10.983 --> 00:06:12.083
Two are healthy.

97
00:06:12.083 --> 00:06:16.418
Two are failing, nine failures each,
with status seventeen twenty-two,

98
00:06:16.418 --> 00:06:20.907
and both come from the third domain
controller, the one that went silent.

99
00:06:20.907 --> 00:06:25.095
The script exits with an error,
because a failing link is a finding.

100
00:06:25.095 --> 00:06:30.690
That record, not the tab, is what Chapter
4 sends to the workspace each morning.

101
00:06:34.440 --> 00:06:39.815
Step 5 checks each table's reachability
on the first morning, from saved answers.

102
00:06:39.815 --> 00:06:44.458
Sign-in logs, audit logs and the probe's
table are late, and the Sentinel health

103
00:06:44.458 --> 00:06:46.226
table is missing altogether:

104
00:06:46.226 --> 00:06:49.690
health monitoring was never
turned on in this workspace.

105
00:06:49.690 --> 00:06:54.453
The servers' heartbeat, event and
security event tables are all delivering.

106
00:06:54.453 --> 00:06:56.365
The script exits with an error.

107
00:06:56.365 --> 00:07:00.703
Step 6 tries to record the first
morning's render as evidence.

108
00:07:00.703 --> 00:07:04.710
The kit refuses: a rendered
view is not evidence.

109
00:07:08.460 --> 00:07:12.385
Step 7 records the reachability
check and the probe instead.

110
00:07:12.385 --> 00:07:17.484
Records one and two, both with result Fail:
each record keeps what its check found,

111
00:07:17.484 --> 00:07:22.197
with continuous monitoring and system
monitoring, and their indicators.

112
00:07:22.197 --> 00:07:25.897
A failing record is still
evidence; it's evidence of the gap.

113
00:07:25.897 --> 00:07:29.749
Step 8 runs this chapter's
tests and the evidence tests:

114
00:07:29.749 --> 00:07:32.420
twenty-seven passed, none failed.

115
00:07:36.170 --> 00:07:40.309
Lab 2 runs the same reachability
check against a test workspace,

116
00:07:40.309 --> 00:07:43.120
never production; the
authors haven't run it.

117
00:07:43.120 --> 00:07:44.991
Sign in to commercial Azure,

118
00:07:44.991 --> 00:07:48.895
add the log analytics extension,
run the check, and sign out.

119
00:07:48.895 --> 00:07:53.957
Every table should be delivering; each
late or missing one becomes a task.

120
00:07:53.957 --> 00:07:56.595
The reachability record
and the probe's record

121
00:07:56.595 --> 00:07:59.707
support continuous monitoring
and system monitoring.

122
00:07:59.707 --> 00:08:03.477
The dangers you saw: a missing
connector that looks healthy,

123
00:08:03.477 --> 00:08:06.093
a missed probe showing yesterday's green,

124
00:08:06.093 --> 00:08:10.170
a silent server read as a quiet one,
and a render filed as evidence.

125
00:08:10.170 --> 00:08:13.080
Next, Chapter 4 deploys the workbook.
